{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And he shall not defile the dwelling-place of YHWH — unclean.</b> If he entered the courtyard, even after immersion without the sprinkling. And its sense is: 'that the dwelling-place of YHWH is unclean'; and likewise (ibid. 71): לכל יבא — 'for all who come'; many such instances occur with missing שי\"ן. And below, when it says כי את מקדש ה׳ טמא, its sense is 'when the sanctuary of YHWH is unclean,' in the language of (Deuteronomy 22:6) כי יקרא קן צפור. And it says משכן even though its sanctity is not eternal sanctity like the eternal House, and it says מקדש even though it was not anointed with the anointing oil like the משכן." "<b>And the Ramban</b>, of blessed memory, explained that here excision (karet) is incurred by one who enters the משכן in impurity even after immersion, since the water of the red heifer ashes was not sprinkled upon him; and below, where it says מקדש, excision is incurred for eating sacrificial flesh in impurity — and excision is already incurred for eating of the flesh of the שלמים offering when his impurity is upon him — and את מקדש ה׳ would be like קדש ה׳, את מקדשו meaning ממנו; and the passage includes the liability of excision for impurity of the Temple and its offerings." "<b>And it is possible</b> to say that משכן and מקדש come to allude to the Temple below, which is a pattern corresponding to the Temple above, set opposite it; and so it says (Exodus 15) מכון לשבתך פעלת יי — 'a habitation for Your dwelling You have made, YHWH' — מכון לשבתך here: the משכן corresponding to the מקדש. And the intention is that he defiles the משכן and the מקדש also, that is, כנסת ישראל, which is the Temple above; and upon this the Rabbi wrote כי את מקדש ה׳ טמא — an allusion to the sanctuary of the sanctuary, that is, to כנסת ישראל, which is a sanctuary to the sanctuary below where He dwells, or to כנסת ישראל, which is a sanctuary to the sanctuary above — and that is the unified Name. And because the sanctuary below corresponds to the one above, therefore it says משכן and מקדש; and likewise it is written (I Kings 8) בנה בניתי בית זבול לך — 'a house have I built, a זבול for You' — a house aligned corresponding to זבול. And a full verse we find (Psalms 122) ירושלים הבנויה כעיר שחוברה לה יחדו. And this is the secret of the language in the name ירושלים, which indicates two, like the language of עינים, אזנים, and similar forms. And the letter יו\"ד that is missing is the Temple above; it is the final ה\"א in the Name. And therefore you will find ה׳ ירושלים written plene in Scripture, to allude that the ה\"א is joined to it at the time of completion."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {2 "The phrase את מקדשו ממנו in comment 1 is opaque; rendered hyper-literally. In comment 2, (שמות טו) is normalized to Exodus 15, (מלכים א ח) to I Kings 8, and (תהלים קכב) to Psalms 122."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:34:07.820053077Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}